cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "GENChase: a single-file studio of seeded scientific simulations, built for print"
message: "If you use GENChase, or a plate made with it, in published work, please cite it and also cite the researchers named in the technique's own credit line."
type: software
authors:
  - name: "Chaos"
    website: "https://github.com/SharpMeow"
repository-code: "https://github.com/SharpMeow/GENChase"
url: "https://github.com/SharpMeow/GENChase"
license-url: "https://github.com/SharpMeow/GENChase/blob/main/LICENSE"
abstract: >-
  GENChase is one HTML file containing one hundred eight pattern-forming systems from
  physics, chemistry, biology and mathematics, each implemented as a real
  simulation rather than as a style applied to noise. Every plate is
  deterministic from a seed, reconstructible from a URL hash, and exportable at
  print resolution in inches, as vectors where the picture is discrete marks.
  Most techniques measure a quantity that theory predicts, from the field
  currently on screen, and print it alongside the value theory gives: the mean
  side count of a two-dimensional froth against Euler's six, unfolded level
  spacing against the Wigner surmise, roughening exponents against the
  Kardar-Parisi-Zhang and Edwards-Wilkinson values, number fluctuations against
  the equilibrium square root. None of the science is original to the project;
  each technique credits the work it implements.
keywords:
  - generative art
  - scientific visualization
  - simulation
  - reaction-diffusion
  - pattern formation
  - WebGL
  - partial differential equations
  - random matrix theory
  - hyperbolic geometry
  - landscape evolution
  - active matter
  - Bose-Einstein condensate
  - cellular Potts model
  - KPZ universality
  - neural field theory
  - print
